language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ csplib_024_langford: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Consider two sets of the numbers from 1 to k. Arrange the 2k numbers in the two sets into a single sequence in which the two 1s appear one number apart, the two 2s appear two numbers apart, and so on, up to the two ks, which appear k numbers apart.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `csplib_024_langford.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/csplib_024_langford.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `csplib_024_langford_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py csplib_024_langford.json
$   savilerow model.eprime csplib_024_langford_1.param -run-solver

$ k: the largest number; the sequence has 2k positions
given k : int(1..)

$ sol[i]: the number at position i of the sequence, positions and numbers 1-based
find sol : matrix indexed by [int(1..2*k)] of int(1..k)
$ pos[v,j]: the position of the j-th copy of the number v (auxiliary)
find pos : matrix indexed by [int(1..k), int(1..2)] of int(1..2*k)

$ search hint
branching on [pos]

such that
$ the two copies of v have v numbers between them: the second copy is v + 1 positions after the first
forAll v : int(1..k) . (pos[v,2] = pos[v,1] + v + 1),
$ every position of the sequence is taken by exactly one copy of a number
allDiff(flatten(pos)),
$ the sequence holds the number v at both copies of v
forAll v : int(1..k) . forAll j : int(1..2) . (sol[pos[v,j]] = v)
